I’ll first inspect the measurement metadata, pattern format, and the framework’s PXRD tooling, then use search/indexing and GSAS-II refinement to build and verify the phase model. I’ll keep the final artifacts internally consistent with the saved `final.gpx`.

The context strongly favors a low-temperature, partially reacted Mn–W–O mixture, but I won’t assume the precursor structures: I’m extracting peak positions and checking the frozen COD search/index resources before deciding how many phases are warranted.

Peak matching identifies two crystalline components with no convincing unexplained strong lines: monoclinic WO₃ (the room-temperature P2₁/c framework) and rutile-type β‑MnO₂ (P4₂/mnm). Their peaks share the expected specimen-dependent angular shift, including the WO₃ 23–25° triplet and MnO₂ lines near 29.0°, 37.7°, 57.0°, and 59.7°. I’m now refining the full profile and testing whether any third phase improves rather than merely overfits.

The quantitative result has stabilized near the stoichiometric unreacted-precursor ratio (about 73 wt% WO₃ / 27 wt% MnO₂). Tests of alternate texture and anisotropic broadening models show only a weak MnO₂ [110] texture and possible c-axis strain anisotropy; WO₃ does not support a meaningful texture term. I’m tightening the atomic-coordinate/background fit now and will retain only effects that are supported and physically bounded.

I’ve converged on a two-phase model: monoclinic WO₃ plus rutile β-MnO₂. I’m now doing the independent project replay and export checks so the CIFs, profile, fractions, and `final.gpx` all come from the same saved refinement.

The saved project replays cleanly on all 8,301 original points without using its stored calculated curve. It reproduces the fit at \(R_\mathrm{wp}=5.479\%\), and the replayed phase inventory gives 72.624 wt% WO₃ and 27.376 wt% β-MnO₂.

The exported CIFs also pass the scorer’s independent occupancy/contact checks, and `result.json` parses as a complete, correctly normalized two-phase inventory. I’m running the final project-to-CIF consistency check now; this catches rounding or export mismatches that a normal fit check can miss.

The fully sandboxed checker cannot start here because this kernel disables unprivileged namespaces. The equivalent native replay already succeeded, so I’m completing the remaining CIF/fraction correspondence check directly against those replay exports.

Completed the two-phase Rietveld analysis:

- Monoclinic WO₃, P 21/c: **72.624 wt%**
- Rutile-type β-MnO₂, P 4₂/mnm: **27.376 wt%**
- Fit: **Rwp 5.479%, Rp 4.041%, GoF 2.039**

The fractions are normalized over modeled crystalline material; amorphous content cannot be quantified. A practical fraction uncertainty is approximately ±2 wt percentage points.

